                "reviews": [
                    {
                        "recommendationid": "77415231",
                        "author": {
                            "steamid": "76561198009334065",
                            "num_reviews": 6,
                            "playtime_forever": 214,
                            "playtime_last_two_weeks": 0,
                            "playtime_at_review": 214,
                            "last_played": 1581893139
                        },
                        "language": "english",
                        "review": "[h1]This is not a deck builder.[/h1]\n[h1]This is a clicker game.[/h1]\n\nLet's deconstruct the short description at the top of this page: \n[code]Book of Demons is a Hack & Slash Deck-building hybrid[/code]\n\n> Hack & Slash\nMovement is on rails. See those paths in the screenshots? Your character is restricted to them and slides along them. Want to swing your weapon at everything in your path? There's some of that, sort of. But then icons appear above enemies that you have to click a bunch to break their shields. Get stunned? Click another icon a bunch. At its heart, this game plays like a clicker game and NOT like Diablo or Torchlight or Path of Exile.\n\n>Deck-building\nThe longer description now says, \"It’s not a card game, but items, spells, and skills are all expressed as cards\". It's as much of a deck builder as any game with an inventory is a deck builder. There is no shuffling. There is no drawing. Cards are just an abstraction over items & skills without any of the gameplay implications you might expect.\n\nIt's easy to see some of the aspects of this game and get a wrong (but appealing) idea of what the game is about. If you're looking for a clicker game with a picture-book version of Diablo 1's plot, then great! But if you were looking for anything remotely like a deck-builder or the gameplay of Diablo, you will be sorely disappointed.",
